WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:24.189
Imagine, for a moment, that

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:29.060
you could travel backwards through time and
give your sixteen year old self the powers

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.320
of a God.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.560
Imagine placing the lives and fates of millions
into your trembling teenage hands.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.170
Would the result be a happier world, a utopia?

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:44.200
Or would the result be catastrophe for everyone
involved, including your younger self?

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:50.360
In October, 54 AD, the Roman Empire made this
little thought experiment a terrifying reality.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.570
They elevated an emotionally unstable 16 year
old boy to the position of emperor.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.250
That boy’s name?

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.250
Nero.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.800
The last of the Julio-Claudian emperors, Nero
transformed the greatest empire the world

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.840
had ever seen into a playground for his darkest
desires.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.140
He had his own mother murdered, ordered Christians
burned alive for entertainment, and indulged

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.550
in perversions so shocking they’d make the
Marquis de Sade blush.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:21.000
Yet he died beloved by Rome’s commoners,
and convinced he was the greatest artist who

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.000
had ever lived.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.440
Join us today as we investigate the life of
the most infamous Roman of all.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:36.310
Family Ties
If you were to take a quick glance at the

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:41.720
background of Nero, you might assume the emperor
had a childhood of unparalleled luxury.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:48.800
Born Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus on December
15, 37 AD, Nero was a member of the Julio-Claudian

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.720
dynasty: AKA the dudes who had been ruling
Rome for decades.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.690
His mother, Julia Agrippina –commonly known
as Agrippina the Younger – was a direct

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.140
descendant of the first emperor, Augustus.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.570
His uncle was the current emperor, Caligula.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.270
His great-uncle was Claudius.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:05.031
But while that might sound like a ticket to
life on Roman easy street, Nero’s mother

00:02:05.031 --> 00:02:07.370
Agrippina could have told him differently.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.420
The Julio-Claudians enjoyed nothing more than
fratricide.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.319
The rot had started way, way back when Agrippina
herself was just a girl, growing up with her

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:16.450
brother Caligula.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.010
As you’ll know if you’ve already watched
our Caligula video, Agrippina’s dad was

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.030
a popular general who was in line for the
imperial throne.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.200
So, naturally, Agrippina’s adopted grandfather,
the emperor Tiberius, had him poisoned in

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.400
19 AD.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.620
It was just the first in a long line of Julio-Claudian
family murders.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.120
Jump ahead to 37 AD.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.530
Tiberius is dead and Agrippina’s brother
Caligula inherits the throne.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.280
At first, things were fine.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.349
It was during this period of calm Agrippina
gave birth to Nero.

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:49.290
But then Caligula had one of his trademark
cuckoo moments and decided Agrippina was plotting

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.290
to kill him.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.569
So he took all her property and banished her
to a tiny island where she could have no contact

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:55.620
with her son.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.570
Dumped with a random aunt, young Nero was
forced to make do with a dancer as a tutor.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:06.239
If that sounds actually kinda OK, jut know
that dancers, actors and poets were considered

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:11.530
the lowest of the low in Roman society, no
better than prostitutes.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:16.750
It seems likely Nero’s aunt felt there was
no point wasting good money on a boy who might

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.180
not live long anyway.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.069
Knowing Caligula’s paranoia, it’d be a
miracle if Nero reached his fifth birthday.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.920
Thank Jupiter, that miracle happened.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.849
On 24 January, 41 AD, the elite Praetorian
Guard assassinated Caligula, stabbing him

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:34.050
to death outside the Palatine Games.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:37.640
They then marched straight to Claudius and
declared him the new emperor.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.090
There was a ‘blink and you’ll miss it’
moment at the start of this video where we

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.660
mentioned that Claudius was Nero’s great
uncle.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.700
Well, that meant Agrippina was Claudius’s
niece.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:51.500
And, unlike Tiberius or Caligula, Emperor
Claudius was all about not killing his relatives.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.300
No sooner had Claudius taken the throne then
he recalled Agrippina from her exile.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:02.129
At last reunited with her son Nero, Agrippina
swore she would never again be left at the

00:04:02.129 --> 00:04:04.810
mercy of her cruel and capricious relatives.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.629
That meant getting her wealth back.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:09.130
That meant building her own powerbase.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:15.739
But how was a penniless widow meant to do
that in a patriarchal society like Rome?

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:17.630
You leave that to Agrippina.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:22.439
First, Agrippina pressured her uncle to find
her a husband, so Claudius forced a consul

00:04:22.439 --> 00:04:23.439
to marry her.

00:04:23.439 --> 00:04:26.759
Next, Agrippina poisoned that consul and inherited
all his wealth.

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:29.939
Then came 48 AD, and Claudius’s execution
of his wife.

00:04:29.939 --> 00:04:35.069
Barely was her body cold than Agrippina was
seducing the emperor’s advisor and giving

00:04:35.069 --> 00:04:36.509
him an excellent plan.

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:40.050
Why didn’t Claudius just marry Agrippina?

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:45.969
The proposal was dressed up as a way of uniting
the two Julio-Claudian branches and ending

00:04:45.969 --> 00:04:47.819
fratricides once and for all.

00:04:47.819 --> 00:04:51.580
But really, it was just an excuse for Agrippina
to get closer to power while Claudius got

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:54.289
to live out his creepy incest fantasies.

00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:58.099
On January 1, 49 AD, Agrippina became empress.

00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:01.319
One year later, Claudius adopted her son,
Nero.

00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:08.240
In ten short years, Agrippina had taken herself
and Nero from pariahs to the heart of imperial

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.240
Rome.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.039
They had money, power.

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:16.020
But never forget that Agrippina was from a
family that prided itself on killing useless

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:17.020
members.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:25.199
And what use did empress Agrippina now have
for her uncle Claudius?

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:32.090
Where the Monster was Nurtured
Come 51 AD, all was not well in Rome’s imperial

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:33.090
palace.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:37.960
That same year, Agrippina had pressured Claudius
into naming Nero his co-heir, alongside the

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.039
emperor’s natural son Britannicus.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:44.779
With Nero suddenly in line for half the throne,
Claudius’s advisors were taking a closer

00:05:44.779 --> 00:05:46.419
look at him.

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:48.669
They didn’t like what they saw.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:53.349
By now a teenager, Nero was a sulky, spoiled
brat who would rather spend his time learning

00:05:53.349 --> 00:05:56.159
to play the lyre than learning how to rule.

00:05:56.159 --> 00:05:59.210
Worse still, the boy was developing a cruel
streak.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.669
One by one, people started to wonder if they
might not have another Caligula on their hands.

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:07.340
Yet they were powerless to stop Agrippina
building her son’s powerbase.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:14.119
In 53 AD, Agrippina forced Nero into marriage
with Claudia Octavia, a daughter of Claudius.

00:06:14.119 --> 00:06:17.039
When we say “forced”, we really mean it.

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:20.339
Claudia Octavia was being perused by another
suitor at the time.

00:06:20.339 --> 00:06:25.129
Agrippina wrecked that courtship and drove
the suitor to suicide.

00:06:25.129 --> 00:06:29.330
Yet even this marriage of convenience wasn’t
enough to convince Agrippina that she was

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.819
safe, that another Caligula wouldn’t come
along and drive her into poverty again.

00:06:33.819 --> 00:06:37.979
The only way to do that would be to make herself
answerable to no-one.

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:42.389
On 13 October, 54 AD, Emperor Claudius suddenly
died.

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:46.849
Ancient sources speculate that Agrippina bribed
the emperor’s taster and slipped him poisoned

00:06:46.849 --> 00:06:47.849
mushrooms.

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:50.949
Whether that’s true or not, the results
were the same.

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:55.479
Control of the empire went to Claudius’s
heirs: Nero and Britannicus.

00:06:55.479 --> 00:06:58.590
Or rather, it should have.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:02.599
At the moment Claudius died, only Nero was
old enough to be considered an adult.

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:06.219
Britannicus was still a child, legally incapable
of ruling.

00:07:06.219 --> 00:07:10.659
So when Agrippina presented Nero alone to
the Praetorian Guard and asked them to salute

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:13.969
their new emperor, there was nothing Britannicus
could do.

00:07:13.969 --> 00:07:20.270
Aged only 16, Nero was elevated to sole ruler
of the world’s most powerful empire.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:25.680
Let’s take a second to imagine how Agrippina
must have felt that day.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.970
Against all the odds, she’d overcome a childhood
of tragedy and an adulthood of exile to install

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:33.809
herself at the heart of Rome.

00:07:33.809 --> 00:07:35.179
She’d murdered her second husband.

00:07:35.179 --> 00:07:37.429
She’d manipulated and killed an emperor.

00:07:37.429 --> 00:07:40.030
And now her son was on the throne.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:41.659
We hope Agrippina savored that moment.

00:07:41.659 --> 00:07:43.439
Really, we do.

00:07:43.439 --> 00:07:46.719
Because it wasn’t destined to last long.

00:07:46.719 --> 00:07:50.459
Just as his mother had started to wonder if
she really needed Claudius, the new Emperor

00:07:50.459 --> 00:07:53.179
Nero was starting to wonder if he really needed
Agrippina.

00:07:53.179 --> 00:07:58.069
Before long, Agrippina found herself being
slowly pushed out the corridors of power.

00:07:58.069 --> 00:08:02.819
Determined to fight, the wily murderess tried
to ally herself with Britannicus, only for

00:08:02.819 --> 00:08:04.849
Nero to have his stepbrother poisoned.

00:08:04.849 --> 00:08:09.059
Not long after, Agrippina was moved out the
palace to a little villa on the outskirts

00:08:09.059 --> 00:08:10.059
of Rome.

00:08:10.059 --> 00:08:14.339
Even the precious marriage she’d secured
for Nero with Claudia Octavia was destroyed,

00:08:14.339 --> 00:08:17.550
as Nero took up instead with Poppaea Sabina.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:22.080
As a last ditch effort, Agrippina made overtures
to Nero’s cousin, suggesting they could

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.199
depose the teenage emperor together.

00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:27.149
But Agrippina’s luck had already run out.

00:08:27.149 --> 00:08:32.740
In early 59 AD, Nero had Agrippina board a
boat that was designed to sink and drown her.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:36.950
When she managed to survive the grueling swim
to shore, her son simply ordered her stabbed

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:37.950
to death.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.030
It’s said that as she realized what was
about to happen, Agrippina pointed at her

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:45.990
womb and screamed “Stab me there, where
the monster had been nurtured!”

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:49.130
Julia Agrippina died on March 23, 59 AD.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:55.410
After the news leaked out, Nero half-assedly
tried to tell everyone it had been a suicide.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.870
But no-one in Rome was under any illusions.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:04.320
The young emperor had broken the greatest
taboo, killing his own mother.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.190
Claudius’s advisors had been wrong.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:08.090
Nero wasn’t the next Caligula.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.110
He was something much, much worse.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:19.150
The Artistic Emperor
You probably have a mental checklist prepared

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:20.400
for the rest of this video.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.380
Fiddling as Rome burns: check.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.080
Persecuting the Christians: check.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.750
Well, don’t worry, we’ll get to those
dubious highlights.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:31.910
But before we tell you about Nero the Crazy
Emperor, we’ve got to tell you about Nero

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.630
the Insanely Popular Emperor.

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:36.710
That’s right: popular.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.370
For all his name may now be synonymous with
decadence, Nero started his reign actually

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:42.490
being quite a cool sorta dude.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:47.250
Prior to Agrippina’s death, Nero had banned
secret trials, cracked down on corruption,

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.080
and handed powers back to the Senate.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.960
He also slashed taxes, banned capital punishment,
granted more rights to slaves, banned animal

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:59.160
bloodsports, and tried to phase out gladiatorial
combat and replace it with Greek wrestling.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.170
So popular were these measures, the great
emperor Trajan would later declare Nero’s

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.420
first five years the greatest reign in Roman
history.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.390
Here we finally reach one of the contradictions
surrounding Nero.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:14.340
Despite all the mad stuff he did, he was beloved
by his subjects.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.730
Nero had a common touch other Roman elites
lacked.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:22.320
After his death, a whole generation of ambitious
provincials would claim to be Nero’s heirs

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.190
as a shortcut to popularity.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:27.830
Why, then, do we only hear about the bad stuff?

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:30.290
Maybe because the common folk didn’t write
the histories.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:34.360
Neither did the ambitious provincials, but
the monied, Roman elite.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.960
And those guys couldn’t stand the way Nero
debased his office.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:44.140
The issue was that, unlike Augustus, Tiberius,
or Caligula, Nero had never wanted to be emperor.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:48.060
It was Agrippina who had schemed him into
the palace, into power.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.320
The other reluctant emperor, Claudius, had
at least been old enough to understand the

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:52.840
responsibilities of office.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:58.380
Nero was young enough to understand the responsibilities
of office could kiss his Roman ass.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:02.670
Not long after Agrippina’s death, Nero talked
openly about giving up the throne to become

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:03.670
a full time poet.

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:07.600
But the power that came with being a living
God was simply too much fun, so instead Nero

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.300
began to live an artist’s life parallel
to his imperial one.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:15.190
The young emperor spent his spare time partying
in brothels, carousing with actors, hanging

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:16.420
out with lowlifes.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:21.260
He liked to get drunk and appear onstage in
theaters, sometimes in female roles.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.930
Remember: the Romans thought artists were
lower than sex workers.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:29.150
Seeing Nero indulge his artistic side for
them was like seeing the Queen of England

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:35.080
dance naked for cash in an Amsterdam brothel.

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:39.520
Although given what happened next, a better
monarchial comparison might be Henry VIII.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:43.310
In 62 AD, Nero’s mistress Poppaea Sabina
became pregnant.

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:48.440
Since Nero was still married to the popular
Claudia Octavia, this caused a scandal.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:53.280
So Nero divorced Claudia and banished her
into exile, an underhand move that was about

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.440
to get even more underhanded.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:59.680
When the people of Rome protested her banishment,
Nero had his ex-wife murdered.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:03.840
It was the second time he’d killed a close
relative and, just as with Agrippina, Nero

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.680
vaguely made out like it had been a suicide.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:44.360
In reality, it was yet another bloodstain
on the artist-emperor’s hands.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:13:09.410
It wouldn’t be the last.

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:32.750
Let the Mother Burn
By July 18, 64 AD, Emperor Nero’s initial

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:35.160
popularity was in terminal nosedive.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:40.360
The same year Nero had Claudia murdered, the
Praetorian Guard prefect Burrus had died.

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:43.970
A natural moderate, he’d kept the emperor’s
darkest desires in check.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.110
So what did Nero do?

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:49.070
He replaced him with a guy called Tigellinus
who thought the best thing the emperor could

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:53.350
do with his dark desires was let them loose.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:54.460
That meant orgies.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.420
Bloody purges of the elite.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:14:00.340
Even fewer hecks given about respecting the
office of emperor.

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:05.220
Come that hot July of 64 AD, the people of
Rome were fed up with their emperor and ready

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.050
to believe anything about him.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:12.370
Into this tinderbox of resentment came a very
real spark.

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:15.170
The causes of the Great Fire of Rome are lost
to history.

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:20.820
All we know is that, on July 18, a conflagration
swept through the Eternal City.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:24.670
At the time, Rome was a collection of wooden
shacks that had been thrown up without any

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:25.690
central plan.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:29.380
So when the inferno got going, it really got
going.

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.010
The fire burned for a week.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:35.680
By the time it fizzled, three of the city’s
districts had been destroyed, and the imperial

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:37.520
palace itself had been damaged.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:41.840
And what was Emperor Nero doing while all
this destruction took place?

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:43.340
That’s absolutely…

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:44.340
Wrong.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:50.550
Yep, the one thing everyone knows about Nero,
that he fiddled as Rome burned, turns out

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.390
to be total bunk.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.970
Nero wasn’t even in the city at the time.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:59.420
Far from regarding the fire as a source of
amusement, Tacitus tells us Nero raced back

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:01.390
to the Rome and spearheaded the relief efforts.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:06.600
He even let hundreds of those left homeless
by the disaster stay in the imperial palace.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:11.590
This kinda raises the question: why do we
all think Nero fiddled as Rome burned?

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:12.910
Two reasons.

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:16.610
One involves a building, the other a Jewish
cult.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:18.830
Let’s tackle the building first.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:22.960
Once the fire was over, Nero pledged to rebuild
Rome grander than before.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:27.970
In most respects, he succeeded, constructing
a new city with streets wide enough to hinder

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:28.970
future fires.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:31.990
However, he also decided to build a massive
palace atop the rubble.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:37.089
A palace that included a 120ft tall statue
of himself.

00:15:37.089 --> 00:15:42.720
For a Roman public that was already leaning
anti-Nero, this was too much.

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:47.040
Rumors started to swirl that Nero had set
the fire himself, specifically so he could

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.740
build his stupid palace.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:53.040
Panicked, Nero decided he needed to find someone
else to blame fast.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.990
His chosen scapegoats were a Jewish sect known
as the Christians.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:03.529
In 64 AD, Rome’s Christians were a small
cult, comprising of non-citizens, refugees,

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:05.500
and Greek-speaking outsiders.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:09.980
Like foreigners, refugees, and immigrants
sadly discover all too frequently, that made

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:11.500
them perfect targets.

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:15.580
On Nero’s orders, prominent Christians were
tortured until they confessed to starting

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:16.580
the fire.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:20.650
Nero then set about having all of them rounded
up and gruesomely executed.

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:24.350
Unlike the tale of the Nero fiddling, all
the stories you’ve heard about his torture

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.620
of the Christians are true.

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:30.370
Christians really were torn apart by wild
animals.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:33.839
Nero really did have them burned alive to
illuminate his garden parties.

00:16:33.839 --> 00:16:36.950
Perhaps it’s no wonder early Christians
came to seriously hate Nero.

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:41.210
There’s a theory that the Antichrist in
the Book of Revelation was meant to symbolize

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:42.210
the emperor.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:45.900
But it wasn’t just the Christians who disliked
Nero’s persecution.

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:49.950
The extreme tortures inflicted were disturbing
even by Roman standards.

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:53.930
In the Senate and the Praetorian Guard, people
began to whisper to one another that maybe

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:56.550
things were getting out of hand.

00:16:56.550 --> 00:17:00.100
That it was time to get rid of their arsonist
emperor.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:05.730
They hadn’t counted on just how hard to
kill Nero would turn out to be.

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:13.399
The Horrors of Love
When people want to prove just what a monster

00:17:13.399 --> 00:17:18.959
Nero was, they usually point to his actions
in 64 AD: allegedly letting Rome burn, and

00:17:18.959 --> 00:17:20.499
actually slaughtering the Christians.

00:17:20.499 --> 00:17:24.329
Really, they should point to the emperor’s
antics the following year.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:31.269
If 64 AD was Nero at his most misunderstood,
65 AD was Nero at his psychotic peak.

00:17:31.269 --> 00:17:34.440
It started when the Senate plot against Nero
was discovered.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:40.280
What followed was a purge that saw 23 conspirators
forced to commit suicide, among them the philosopher

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:41.280
Seneca.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:46.649
But if 23 dead doesn’t sound so bad, know
that this was just the warm up act.

00:17:46.649 --> 00:17:50.249
In the wake of the conspiracy, Nero brought
back the treason trials that had been such

00:17:50.249 --> 00:17:53.720
a feature of Tiberius and Caligula’s rules.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:58.059
Kangaroo courts that convicted and executed
people on a whim, the treason trials were

00:17:58.059 --> 00:18:03.019
infamous for being an underhand way for the
emperor to get rid of anyone he didn’t like.

00:18:03.019 --> 00:18:08.759
And Nero in 65 AD had an enemies list longer
than War and Peace.

00:18:08.759 --> 00:18:12.580
Yet even this bloodshed doesn’t compare
to Nero’s cruelest act that year.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:17.230
At some point that summer, Nero got in a blazing
row with his wife, Poppaea Sabina.

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:18.570
Remember her?

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:22.100
The mistress who got pregnant, causing Nero
to murder his previous wife?

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:25.519
Well, Poppaea probably should have realized
that ordering your wife’s assassination

00:18:25.519 --> 00:18:28.389
isn’t a great sign of mental stability.

00:18:28.389 --> 00:18:32.150
According to Tacitus, Nero, mad with rage,
kicked Poppaea to death.

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:37.110
The poor girl was pregnant at the time, and
Nero made sure to aim his vicious kicks at

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:38.440
her swollen stomach.

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.100
In the aftermath of this brutal double murder,
Nero went insane.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:46.490
He had Poppaea stuffed and embalmed and her
corpse deposited in a mausoleum so he could

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:47.919
visit it whenever he wanted.

00:18:47.919 --> 00:18:51.970
Freakier still, Nero caught sight of a 13
year old boy a few weeks later who was the

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:53.830
spitting image of his murdered wife.

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:58.009
Known as Sporus, this unlucky boy was kidnapped
by Nero’s men and castrated.

00:18:58.009 --> 00:19:02.590
Nero then forced Sporus to dress in clothes
belonging to the dead Poppaea, and married

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:04.620
him in a bizarre service.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:08.690
After that, Sporus could be seen, dressed
and acting like the dead empress, sat at Nero’s

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:11.690
side as the deranged emperor kissed and fondled
him.

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:16.330
If this wasn’t already creepy enough, Nero
himself took to wearing a Poppaea mask when

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:21.720
he performed onstage, embodying the girl whose
life he’d so viciously snatched away.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:27.200
Yet even as he drifted into Norman Bates territory,
Nero didn’t lose his common touch.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:32.230
The next year, 66 AD, he left on a tour of
Rome’s eastern provinces, effectively giving

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:34.429
himself a year’s break from running the
empire.

00:19:34.429 --> 00:19:39.169
But rather than react to this creepy murderer
the way the elites in Rome had, those in the

00:19:39.169 --> 00:19:42.249
east acted like Nero was an ancient rock star.

00:19:42.249 --> 00:19:44.870
The thing was, Nero knew how to play a crowd.

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:48.850
In Greece, he dressed like an ascetic, with
bare feet and flowing hair, and took an active

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:50.519
interest in the country’s new religions.

00:19:50.519 --> 00:19:55.720
Wherever he went, he granted towns “free
city” status, ensuring the inhabitants celebrated

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:56.720
him.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:59.159
He was so popular that he even took part in
the Olympic Games.

00:19:59.159 --> 00:20:02.179
Unsurprisingly, he won every event he entered.

00:20:02.179 --> 00:20:05.679
It seems Nero himself felt being in the east
was a form of freedom.

00:20:05.679 --> 00:20:09.400
He began appearing on stage more and more,
often playing the roles of pregnant women.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:13.619
When word got back to Rome, upper society
was more scandalized than ever.

00:20:13.619 --> 00:20:18.809
But Nero was wrong if he thought he could
just keep violating norms and doing whatever

00:20:18.809 --> 00:20:20.029
the hell he felt like.

00:20:20.029 --> 00:20:25.549
The emperor didn’t know it yet, but his
days on this earth were already numbered.

00:20:25.549 --> 00:20:33.179
“What an Artist Dies in Me”
In early 68 AD, Nero sent an order to the

00:20:33.179 --> 00:20:36.970
governor of Gaul, a guy called Vindex, telling
him to raise local taxes.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:41.480
It was just one of many such orders Nero was
in the habit of sending out to fund his lavish

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:42.480
lifestyle.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.110
But this time something was different.

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:47.320
Nero didn’t know it, but Vindex was at breaking
point.

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:51.690
Fed up with endlessly having to raise taxes
on Gaul, Vindex snapped when he saw Nero’s

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:52.690
latest demand.

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.429
He took up arms and declared himself in rebellion
against the emperor.

00:20:56.429 --> 00:20:57.730
But then he did a funny thing.

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:02.389
He declared allegiance to the popular governor
of Spain, a guy named Galba.

00:21:02.389 --> 00:21:05.710
This was funny because Galba was very much
not in rebellion.

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:09.929
When he heard of Vindex’s declaration, he
freaked out and declared himself for Nero.

00:21:09.929 --> 00:21:14.799
But just hearing Vindex mention Galba was
enough to send Nero’s paranoia into overdrive.

00:21:14.799 --> 00:21:17.869
He had both men declared enemies of Rome.

00:21:17.869 --> 00:21:21.899
Realizing he was doomed either way, Galba
went “ah, screw it” and declared he was

00:21:21.899 --> 00:21:23.239
in rebellion after all.

00:21:23.239 --> 00:21:27.440
Unfortunately for Nero, a popular governor
leading a rebellion was exactly the news Rome

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:28.679
had been waiting for.

00:21:28.679 --> 00:21:32.880
As news of Galba’s revolt hit, the Praetorian
Guard came out in support of him.

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:33.950
As did the Senate.

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:36.320
As did a huge swathe of the army.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:38.639
As did… well, everyone.

00:21:38.639 --> 00:21:43.249
On the night of June 9, 68 AD, Nero woke up
to find his palace empty.

00:21:43.249 --> 00:21:46.860
Sensing the end was near, everyone had deserted
the emperor.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:51.690
As Nero ran through the empty, cavernous rooms,
he screamed first for help, and then for someone

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:52.690
to kill him.

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:57.899
When no voices came in reply, Nero is said
to have wailed “have I neither friend nor

00:21:57.899 --> 00:21:58.899
foe?”

00:21:58.899 --> 00:22:00.009
But the emperor was wrong.

00:22:00.009 --> 00:22:01.899
He had plenty of foes.

00:22:01.899 --> 00:22:04.870
Across the city, the Senate had called an
emergency meeting.

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:08.869
Seeing which way the wind was blowing, they
declared Nero an enemy of Rome and dispatched

00:22:08.869 --> 00:22:10.210
soldiers to finish him off.

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:12.559
But Nero was no longer in his palace.

00:22:12.559 --> 00:22:17.590
Accompanied by a handful of slaves and one
freedman, Nero had fled to a darkened villa

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:19.739
four miles outside the Eternal City.

00:22:19.739 --> 00:22:24.039
Had he been able to make it to the east of
the empire, where he was still popular, he

00:22:24.039 --> 00:22:25.309
may have stood a chance.

00:22:25.309 --> 00:22:30.159
But that chance slipped through his fingers,
and now there was only one way this could

00:22:30.159 --> 00:22:31.159
end.

00:22:31.159 --> 00:22:34.779
As the soldiers closed in on his hiding place,
Nero is said to have started muttering to

00:22:34.779 --> 00:22:37.610
himself “what an artist dies in me!”

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:40.039
He demanded his slaves kill him, but to no
avail.

00:22:40.039 --> 00:22:44.200
Finally, with the soldiers right outside and
dawn finally breaking, Nero snatched a dagger

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:47.249
from someone and plunged it deep into his
own throat.

00:22:47.249 --> 00:22:51.559
The soldiers stormed in to find the emperor
knelt on the floor, his tunic soaked with

00:22:51.559 --> 00:22:52.559
blood.

00:22:52.559 --> 00:22:56.640
With what we imagine must have been an unhinged
grin on his face, Nero supposedly delivered

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:00.610
one last, immortal line to the men sent to
kill him:

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:01.980
“Too late.”

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:06.909
Emperor Nero died that morning, unloved and
alone, a pariah to his people.

00:23:06.909 --> 00:23:11.009
With his death, the Julio-Claudian line that
had ruled Rome since it became an empire was

00:23:11.009 --> 00:23:12.009
snuffed out.

00:23:12.009 --> 00:23:16.090
The fratricidal family that had given the
world the highs of Augustus and the lows of

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:19.179
Caligula would produce no more emperors.

00:23:19.179 --> 00:23:24.220
But if anyone was expecting Nero’s death
to herald an era of stability, they were in

00:23:24.220 --> 00:23:26.269
for a rude awakening.

00:23:26.269 --> 00:23:29.179
In the wake of Nero’s suicide, Galba was
made emperor.

00:23:29.179 --> 00:23:32.440
But he ruled a mere six months before being
assassinated.

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:34.989
Galba’s death would plunge the empire into
chaos.

00:23:34.989 --> 00:23:40.200
69 AD would see four emperors come and go
before the dust settled and Vespasian claimed

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:41.200
the throne.

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:43.019
But all of that is for another video.

00:23:43.019 --> 00:23:44.130
What of Nero?

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:46.519
What of the man we came here to discuss?

00:23:46.519 --> 00:23:51.149
Although he’s infamous today as the most
decadent emperor, you could argue that Nero

00:23:51.149 --> 00:23:52.249
wasn’t the worst.

00:23:52.249 --> 00:23:55.269
Sure, he did some terrible, inhuman things.

00:23:55.269 --> 00:24:00.659
But even his cruelest acts weren’t the outright
sadism of Caligula, as much as they were the

00:24:00.659 --> 00:24:05.289
lashings out of an angry teenage boy who never
mentally grew up.

00:24:05.289 --> 00:24:08.799
It’s true that Nero was a nasty man and
an unhinged pervert.

00:24:08.799 --> 00:24:13.129
But it’s also true that his badness is sometimes
exaggerated.

00:24:13.129 --> 00:24:15.340
He never fiddled while Rome burned.

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:18.850
The first years of his rule were even something
of a golden age.

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:23.169
While it’s fitting that we remember the
awful, vicious side of Nero, it’s also worth

00:24:23.169 --> 00:24:27.549
remembering that history is more nuanced than
it often appears.

00:24:27.549 --> 00:24:32.749
Nero was deranged, but hopefully in our video
today we’ve shown there was more to him

00:24:32.749 --> 00:24:53.559
than simply being Rome’s crazy emperor.

